# Pulse architecture

```yaml
architecture:
  summary: Pulse is a complete local-first predictive traffic-surge platform for a protected commerce workload. A FastAPI agent combines scheduled prewarming and real-time acceleration/leading-signal detection, serializes both through one audited priority arbiter and response pipeline, manages gradual recovery and durable reconciliation, and optionally executes bounded AWS Auto Scaling changes. A second FastAPI service owns reversible load shedding while keeping checkout normal, PostgreSQL stores control/evidence state, Locust drives deterministic scenarios, and a read-only Next.js dashboard consumes versioned operator APIs.

  style: multi-service-monorepo

  layers:
    - name: http
      paths:
        - agent/app/main.py
        - agent/app/api
        - demo_app/app/main.py
        - demo_app/app/routes
        - demo_app/app/schemas.py
        - dashboard/app
        - dashboard/components
        - dashboard/lib
    - name: business
      paths:
        - agent/app/detection
        - agent/app/orchestration
        - agent/app/services
        - agent/app/workers
        - demo_app/app/shedding
        - demo_app/app/metrics.py
        - common
        - load_tests/scenarios
        - load_tests/scripts
    - name: infrastructure
      paths:
        - agent/app/aws
        - agent/app/metrics/providers
        - agent/app/runtime.py
        - db/models
        - db/repositories
        - db/migrations
        - db/session.py
        - infra
        - scripts
        - agent/Dockerfile
        - demo_app/Dockerfile
        - dashboard/Dockerfile
        - docker-compose.yml

  boundaries:
    - The demo application is authoritative for endpoint policy; the agent changes tiers only through the authenticated HTTP boundary
    - Critical endpoint policy is centralized and checkout bypasses every limiter, cache fallback, disable rule, and protection tier
    - Scheduled and real-time commands share one cross-mode priority arbiter, response pipeline, state machine, recovery policy, and ASG adapter
    - Capacity and tier intent is claimed durably before mutation; an atomic tier outbox, bounded retry queue, and reconciliation resume incomplete work without repeating capacity
    - Database access is isolated behind asynchronous repositories and session factories; mandatory DB or worker failure degrades readiness to 503 while polling tasks remain alive and recover in process
    - Flexible bounded evidence is JSONB, while dashboard, evaluation, capacity, state, idempotency, and lifecycle fields remain typed columns
    - The browser performs bounded credential-free GET polling and consumes explicit pulse.snapshot.v1 DTOs; it never receives the control token
    - Dry-run constructs no AWS clients; live SetDesiredCapacity is target-scoped, double-clamped, idempotent, and direction/intent aware
    - Recovery is the only decrease path, requires distinct sustained-low evidence, applies one bounded step, and retains provider cooldown while protective scale-out bypasses it
    - Collection buffers, API windows/pages, retries, reconciliation, retention, evidence, and logs are bounded

  integrations:
    - PostgreSQL 16 for snapshots, predictions/points, scaling actions and tier outbox, retries, scheduled events, demo runs, and shedding intervals
    - Protected demo-app HTTP metrics and authenticated load-shedding control APIs
    - Optional CloudWatch regional ASG CPU and us-east-1 CloudFront request signals
    - Optional SQS queue-depth and HTTP session/login leading signals
    - AWS Auto Scaling read/SetDesiredCapacity through one bounded boto3 adapter
    - Docker Compose for PostgreSQL migration, demo app, agent/workers, dashboard, and Locust orchestration
    - Next.js dashboard for read-only status/history/results visualization
    - GitHub Actions for Python/PostgreSQL/dashboard/Compose/Locust/Terraform/secret delivery verification
    - Terraform for an optional IMDSv2 launch template, bounded ASG, CloudWatch comparator alarm, and least-privilege runtime role

  auth:
    userFacingPattern: The demo commerce endpoints are intentionally unauthenticated for local load testing
    partnerPattern: Agent simulated-signal/demo-run mutations and demo-app load-shedding control require X-Pulse-Control-Token with constant-time comparison; operator/dashboard reads are intentionally credential-free for trusted local or network-restricted use
    configPaths:
      - .env.example
      - agent/app/config.py
      - agent/app/api/internal.py
      - demo_app/app/config.py
      - demo_app/app/routes/operations.py

  errorHandling:
    businessException: FastAPI emits structured error codes for validation/query/control failures; provider text is sanitized, persistence failure prevents unaudited mutation, and dependency/worker degradation is exposed as health 503 while bounded loops retry
    codes:
      - HTTP 401 for invalid internal control token
      - HTTP 404 for missing run/prediction results
      - HTTP 409 for idempotency, active-run, or authoritative tier conflicts
      - HTTP 422 for invalid request payloads
      - HTTP 429 for intentionally rate-limited non-critical traffic
      - HTTP 500 for sanitized bounded operator query failures or unexpected demo failures counted by traffic metrics
      - HTTP 503 for persistence, dependency, mandatory worker, or demo-control unavailability
      - UNKNOWN action status for ambiguous provider outcomes pending reconciliation

  diagrams:
    existing:
      - README.md architecture overview
      - docs/architecture.md runtime, data, persistence, and failure boundaries
      - docs/state-machine.md executable control-state and response sequence
    preferredFormat: mermaid

  repositoryRelationships:
    - primary: Suryavarma333/pulse-engine
      consumes:
        - PostgreSQL 16
        - protected demo-app HTTP APIs
        - AWS Auto Scaling and CloudWatch when live mode is explicitly enabled
        - optional CloudFront, SQS, and session-signal sources
```
